<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.10.4: Using the Standard Form of an Equation to Find the X- and Y-Intercepts</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01910</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.10.4: Using the Standard Form of an Equation to Find the X- and Y-Intercepts</md:title>
    <md:uuid>395ce4a5-cb02-477b-89d5-e33632ae1bab</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>

<para>The equations \(y=x^2+6x+8\) and \(y=(x+2)(x+4)\) both define the same quadratic function.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Without graphing, identify the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph. Explain how you know.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((-2,0)\),\((-4,0)\); The constants in the factors of an equation in factored form give a clue about where the \(x\)-intercepts are when the equation is graphed. In the equation \(y=(x+2)(x+4)\), the \(x\)-intercepts are \((-2,0)\) and \((-4,0)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Without graphing, identify the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept of the graph. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>8; The \(y\)-intercepts of the graph can be seen when the equation is in standard form. The \(y\)-coordinate is the number without the variable. In the equation \(y=x^2+6x+8\), it is the 8 so the \(y\)-intercept is \((0,8)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
